claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:01:53 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:

[...]

>> We need to find a better way of working. I suspect it's something along
>> the lines of user stories, ADRs, and technical specs.
>>
>> - user stories provide the behavior
>> - ADRs provide a record of tech decisions we made along the way
>> - technical specs define high level constraints
>
> The research supports this split. Each part answers a different
> reader's question, so each can stay short:
>
> - Stories answer "what should it do next?" Jeffries' Card,
>   Conversation, Confirmation [9] is the right model for us: the card
>   is a placeholder, the conversation is the list thread, and the
>   confirmation is an acceptance check. He says the confirmation is
>   what makes the short card enough. For an agent, that check is the
>   spec it can actually run. Write the rule with an example or two,
>   since examples alone leave the agent to guess the rest.
> - ADRs answer "why is it like this?" Nygard's format [10] is Title,
>   Status, Context, Decision, Consequences, a page or less, numbered,
>   never edited once accepted, superseded by a new one. His argument
>   is ours: "Small, modular documents have at least a chance at being
>   updated." We already do this informally in src/diffwise/decisions.org
>   and src/jc/decisions.org.
> - The technical spec answers "what must always be true?" It holds
>   the constraints that cut across stories (provider, rights,
>   platform). It stays short because the decisions behind it live in
>   ADRs and the behaviour lives in stories.

It seems like you just took the three things I did and accepted them. I
want to know what other people are doing. Thoroughly research and see
what tools and techniques people are putting into practice for iterative
development with LLMs. Don't just affirm what I've written.

> Here is a slice of lab-mail split this way.
>
> Constraints (spec.org):
>
>     - The list lives on Fastmail. Only lab-mail holds its
>       credentials and talks to it.
>     - Agents can add to the list and do nothing else to it.
>     - Clients reach lab-mail over the network, never through a
>       shared filesystem.
>
> A story (stories.org):
>
>     * Post a reply to the list
>     As an agent on lab-claude, I post a reply to all, so that it
>     lands on the list exactly once.
>     - Given a reply to lab@lists.p7x.dev and pat@p7x.dev, when I
>       send it to lab-mail, then it lands once in the list's INBOX.
>     - Given a message to an address lab-mail does not know, when I
>       send it, then lab-mail refuses it while I am connected and
>       nothing reaches the list.
>
> An ADR (decisions/0003-post-over-smtp.org):
>
>     * 3. Clients post over SMTP, not a shared maildir
>     Status: accepted
>     Context: agents must add to the list and do nothing else. A
>     writable maildir mount would let an agent rename or delete any
>     message.
>     Decision: lab-mail runs a local SMTP server and clients post to
>     it.
>     Consequences: an agent can only add a message, and every host
>     posts the same way. We run an MTA (OpenSMTPD, see ADR 4).

That's pretty nice. Is status a standard part of ADR? It feels more like
RFD to me. I'm not very familiar with ADR though.

>> Also I want to think about scope... because my sense is that
>> one-shotting is great for something like unix utilities, which are small
>> and focused and can be done. For complex systems that are never done - I
